{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff HEAD~1 HEAD; git rev-parse HEAD; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\nindex a2c9033b..5397c34a 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\n@@ -5,7 +5,8 @@ discussion recorded in\n `public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\n Part Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n `collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n-(thirteen findings, all addressed below, and six answers adopted in §10).\n+(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight findings; all addressed\n+below, and nine answers adopted in §10).\n Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n [`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\n and to the version-2 amendment\n@@ -65,10 +66,12 @@ distinction the amendment draws between its versions and executable\n code.\n \n This document tables the **semantic** profile's registers, frames and\n-closures in full. The stack, register and walker profiles use the same\n-carrier with their own `R`, `F`, layout and free-env rows, to be tabled\n-in a follow-up against amendment §5.2–5.4; the carrier admits them by\n-profile but this draft does not yet specify their rows. Like version 2,\n+closures in full, and **version 3 declares support for the semantic\n+profile only**: a version-3 body whose profile map names `:stack`,\n+`:register` or `:walker` fails closed at the profile gate\n+(`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) until those profiles' row tables and\n+validation rules are published against amendment §5.2–5.4, with their\n+code addresses and profile maps unchanged. Like version 2,\n version 3 transports native suspended state within a profile and does not\n translate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\n implements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\n@@ -81,9 +84,29 @@ Every row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n `[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n as for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n \n-**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`, the canonical\n-CBOR of the body under the established digest. Equal bodies are one row.\n-The id is Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n+**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\n+something to run on before any row is interpreted:\n+\n+```clojure\n+{:yin.k/handoff true\n+ :yin.k/version 3\n+ :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n+ :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n+```\n+\n+Unlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\n+any traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so the gate's walk over install\n+children is over a map from id to one row and is bounded by a visited\n+set; a cref that leaves the rows, or a child graph that revisits a row,\n+stops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable` rather than looping or choosing\n+arbitrary data. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what is\n+hashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in `:rows`\n+is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n+\n+**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: the digest\n+named by the address's own multihash prefix over the canonical CBOR of\n+the body, never an unspecified default. Equal bodies are one row. The id\n+is Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n \n **Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n in the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n@@ -115,7 +138,7 @@ root and per install child.\n   census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n-  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 4), because a receiver\n+  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 5), because a receiver\n   validates, it does not trust construction.\n - *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n@@ -147,24 +170,45 @@ can never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n is the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n \n **The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n-arms, closed under nesting: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n-(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, strings, keywords,\n-symbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys\n-and values are in the domain*. Scalar and collection carrier classes are\n+arms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n+`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n+(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n+Rational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n+symbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n+values and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n retained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n are not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n-kind `:byte-string`. Metadata: a literal travels with whatever metadata\n-Jing's canonical encoding preserves for it, hashed as Jing hashes it; a\n-collection that must become a row (because it reaches a reference) has\n-no metadata slot, and one that carries metadata refuses as\n-`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the path. A guest map is\n-always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open data inside the\n-wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n-\n-A collection whose elements include a reference is not a literal; it\n-becomes a row (§5.1) and the position holds `[:row B]`. This is the only\n-structural decision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question,\n-*does this collection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n+kind `:byte-string`.\n+\n+**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n+portability and whether a value reaches a reference: retained metadata\n+that holds a cursor reference, a closure, or any non-literal makes its\n+carrier non-literal, and since collection rows have no metadata slot,\n+such a value refuses as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the\n+path (copying it as data would carry source coordinates and seals\n+unremapped). Reference-free retained metadata on a literal travels as\n+Jing preserves it and is hashed as Jing hashes it. Metadata a reader\n+strips is not retained metadata and plays no part.\n+\n+A guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n+data inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n+\n+A collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\n+reference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\n+holds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\n+and **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n+`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\n+collections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\n+decision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\n+collection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n+\n+**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n+§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n+guest literals, namely install responses, request and link envelopes,\n+operation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data slot holds plain\n+canonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that governs it (an\n+install response by the linker's delivery checks, an op-id by §7.4.3\n+r5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these slots `D`.\n \n Positions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n names, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n@@ -181,7 +225,13 @@ Merkle id.\n \n `N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n code address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n-`S` a symbol.\n+`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\n+only the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n+of another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n+`:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\n+have unique keys under canonical bytes **and** under portable decoded\n+equality, since Jing's own map and set rules do not apply to a key list\n+spelled as a vector.\n \n ### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n \n@@ -202,7 +252,10 @@ code address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n `:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n §6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\n receiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\n-reconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`.\n+reconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's\n+registers are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\n+must be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\n+safepoint.\n \n ### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n \n@@ -235,14 +288,17 @@ the amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n | reason | slots after the reason |\n |---|---|\n | `:next` | `cell-C` |\n-| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id` |\n-| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id  op` (op may be nil) |\n-| `:ffi-request` | `call-id  request-envelope-V  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id` |\n-| `:link-request` | `link-id  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-V  op-id` |\n-| `:link-response` | `link-id  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n+| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n+| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n+| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  request-envelope-D  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n+| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n+| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n | `:install` | `name-S` |\n \n-`op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n+Correlation must agree, not merely be present: a cell's `:cell-body`\n+stream is the pending's response stream; an envelope's link id or call\n+id equals the pending's; a `:link-request` envelope names the pending's\n+module. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n present exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n writer, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n and subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n@@ -261,7 +317,7 @@ is `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n | `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n | `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n | `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n-| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent  response-V  child-cref` |\n+| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n | `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n | `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n \n@@ -271,8 +327,14 @@ M4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n snapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n reachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n §7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n-contract permits), the verified response verbatim as a `V`, and the\n-child as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role.\n+contract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\n+child as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\n+means verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\n+the response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\n+id equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\n+is delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\n+contract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\n+amendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n \n ### 5.5 The root\n \n@@ -303,14 +365,23 @@ neither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n never collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n ordered waits.\n \n-`custody` is the version-1 header carried as plain data, under the same\n-rules (§7.2.1, amendment §3): on a `:root` of kind `:blocked` or\n-`:parked` under `:yin.k/exclusive`, exactly `{:yin.k/policy\n-:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/origin :yin.k/next-op-seq}`\n-with origin absent on a first export; a fork root carries `{:yin.k/policy\n-:yin.k/fork}` and nothing else; a `:halted` root carries origin only; a\n-`:child` carries nil and is validated with its root's occurrence, origin\n-and counter passed down. No body carries an epoch.\n+`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n+§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n+\n+| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n+|---|---|---|---|\n+| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n+| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n+| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n+| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n+| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n+\n+The explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n+empty-header fork representation: a version-3 fork root is admitted\n+exactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and is never upgraded\n+to custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n+`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n+unchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n \n The root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n address**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n@@ -322,38 +393,60 @@ the carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n ## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n \n Cell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n-two root ids. The numbering is computed **before** any row is hashed and\n-without reference to any hash, in one pass:\n-\n-1. **Traversal order.** Walk the task in this fixed order, recursing\n-   into every reachable structure: frames in wait order, and within a\n-   wait its registers then its pending; within registers, env (bindings\n-   by sorted symbol, then store-of's module store if not yet walked),\n-   stack bottom-up, k bottom-to-top with each frame's env; the parked\n-   table by sorted `P`; the store by sorted key skeleton; module stores\n-   by sorted `M`; the result; install entries by sorted module name,\n-   each child recursively as its own task with its own numbering.\n-   Within a value: vectors and lists in order; sets and maps by the\n-   element's or key's **cell-free skeleton**, the value's canonical bytes\n-   with every cell reference replaced by the constant `[:cell nil]`;\n-   closures by segment, entry, then bindings; pendings in slot order.\n-2. **Assignment.** The first time a cell is reached it receives the next\n-   `n`; a cell reached again keeps its number. Distinct cells at equal\n-   streams and positions are distinct and receive distinct numbers.\n-3. **Ties.** Two elements of a set, or two keys of a map, with equal\n-   skeletons are ordered by the canonical bytes of their referenced\n-   cells' *bodies* (stream marker, then position), walked in the\n-   element's own traversal order. Two elements that are equal in\n-   skeleton and in every referenced cell body are symmetric: either\n-   assignment produces the same row set and the same root id, so the\n-   choice is immaterial and the encoder takes them in encounter order.\n-\n-Because skeletons omit cell numbers and ties are broken by cell bodies,\n-the ordering never depends on a number the pass has yet to assign. Rows\n-are then built and hashed with the numbers fixed. The receiver\n-**recomputes the numbering from the decoded rows and refuses**\n-(`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`) if the carried ids\n-differ from the recomputed ones; contiguity alone is not canonicality.\n+two root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cell's number must depend on\n+everything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n+elsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n+holding cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n+looks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n+can). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n+topology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n+which any algorithm is checked.\n+\n+**Definition.** Let the task's cells be `n` in number. For each bijection\n+`σ` from the cells to `{1 … n}`, build the complete row set of §5 with\n+cell `c` spelled `:yin.k/c-σ(c)` (ordered containers in order; sets,\n+maps, stores, bindings and tables sorted by the canonical bytes of their\n+keys *as spelled under σ*), and take the canonical bytes of the root\n+body. The canonical numbering is the `σ` whose root bytes are least\n+under bytewise order. Two `σ` with equal least bytes are automorphisms\n+of the task (they produce identical row sets by construction) and the\n+choice between them is immaterial by definition, not by assumption.\n+\n+**Algorithm.** The lift computes the same `σ` without enumerating all\n+`n!` bijections:\n+\n+1. **Refinement.** Give every cell the signature of its body (stream,\n+   position). Repeat: encode the task with each cell spelled by its\n+   current signature class, in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within\n+   a wait registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack\n+   bottom-up, k bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key;\n+   module stores by `M`; result; installs by name with each child as\n+   its own task), and give each cell the new signature *(old class, the\n+   multiset of positions at which it is referenced, where a position is\n+   the path from the root with unordered containers' elements named by\n+   their own current encoding)*. Stop when the partition into classes\n+   stops changing. This is colour refinement over the reference graph\n+   and terminates in at most `n` rounds.\n+2. **Individualization.** If every class is a singleton, order classes\n+   by their signature's canonical bytes and assign `1 … n`. Otherwise\n+   pick the smallest class under that order, try each of its members as\n+   the next individualized cell, refine again, and recurse; among the\n+   resulting complete numberings take the one with the least root\n+   bytes. Residual classes after full refinement are orbits of the\n+   task's automorphism group, so every choice within a class yields the\n+   same root bytes and the search is bounded by the symmetry of the\n+   state, which for a parked task is small.\n+\n+Skeletons used inside the refinement keep the full value-union tags:\n+a cell position is spelled `[:cell <class>]`, never a bare class, so it\n+cannot collide with `[:lit [:cell …]]` (§10.9). Context lookups (parked\n+ids, store-of) are not expanded during refinement; they are followed\n+once as edges to their target rows, which are already in the traversal.\n+\n+**Validation.** The receiver recomputes `σ` from the decoded rows and\n+refuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if the\n+carried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality. This\n+is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind.\n \n Under this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n different states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n@@ -388,53 +481,74 @@ occurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n Validation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n \n-1. **Gate.** Decode canonical bytes retaining numeric classes. The root\n-   row's kind is `:task` and its version is the exact integer 3, else\n-   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported set). Traverse\n-   install children in canonical module-name order; gate every child's\n-   version, then every profile map against the registry, then require\n-   all versions 3 and all profiles equal to the root's. A malformed\n-   install container is `:yin.k/undecodable`; nothing claims to have\n-   gated a non-task.\n+1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n+   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n+   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n+   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n+   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n+   root in canonical module-name order with a visited set, following\n+   only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the rows, a revisit, or a\n+   non-`:task` child stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`. Gate every\n+   child's version, then every profile map against the registry (only\n+   the semantic profile is supported, §2), then require all versions 3\n+   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n+   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n+   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n 2. **Structure.** Every row's kind is tabled with the right arity; every\n-   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `V` is in §4's union and\n-   every literal in the portable domain; no two rows share an id; exactly\n-   one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5 hold; the custody slot\n-   matches the role, kind and policy.\n-3. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the Jing address of its\n-   body; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\n-   under the stamp; the requested body address, if one was given, equals\n-   the root id. Mismatch is `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or\n-   code key.\n-4. **Graph.** Every cref names a present row of a kind admissible in that\n-   slot; the structural edge family is acyclic; every row is reachable\n-   from the root (an unreachable row is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every\n-   `[:cell C]` names a census entry and every census entry is reached,\n-   per task and per child; every `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves\n-   in the parked table; every `store-of` resolves to a carried module\n-   store; every `:install` pending has its entry and every entry's child\n-   is a `:child` task.\n-5. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare (kind\n-   `:noncanonical-cells` on mismatch).\n-6. **Code and frames.** Each resume pc is a static safepoint whose kinds\n-   admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/reason-mismatch`); a parked record's\n-   pc is an explicit-park safepoint; return pcs follow a non-tail call;\n-   stack bases are bounded and nondecreasing; closure params agree with\n-   the addressed lambda; integer slots are in range.\n-7. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; every op-id\n-   below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without origin;\n-   link and FFI correlation fields present.\n-8. **Dependency closure.** `requires` evaluated per UCF §7.6.5: missing\n-   segments, primitives, modules, streams, cursor profiles are\n-   `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body with cells claims\n-   `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is complete for every\n-   `store-of` reached.\n-9. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n-   operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n-   never adds missing header keys.\n-10. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n+   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `[:row B]` targets a row\n+   of a kind admissible in that position; every `V` is in §4's union,\n+   every literal in the portable domain, every `D` in the canonical\n+   bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5\n+   hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy table.\n+3. **Canonical form.** Every sorted slot is in its tabled order; every\n+   keyed row has unique keys under canonical bytes and under portable\n+   decoded equality; optional absence has its one spelling (nil in the\n+   tabled slot); a reference-free collection is spelled `[:lit d]`, never\n+   a row; empty collections are literals; metadata obeys §4. A breach is\n+   `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row and slot. Without this step two\n+   different valid encodings could lower to one state, or a `:bindings`\n+   row could carry one symbol twice and lose a binding on lower.\n+4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the address of its body\n+   under the digest the id's multihash names; every code payload hashes\n+   to its key `A` and is well formed under the stamp; the requested body\n+   address, if one was given, equals the root id. Mismatch is\n+   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or code key.\n+5. **Graph.** The structural edge family is acyclic; every row is\n+   reachable from the root through structural edges (an unreachable row\n+   is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a census entry and\n+   every census entry is reached **through the task's own roots, the\n+   census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and per child; every\n+   `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked table; every\n+   `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every `:install`\n+   pending has its entry and every entry's child is a `:child` task.\n+6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare\n+   (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`).\n+7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n+   whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n+   `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n+   safepoint; a reified value's pc is a `:current-continuation`\n+   successor; return pcs follow a non-tail call; stack bases are bounded\n+   and nondecreasing; closure params agree with the addressed lambda;\n+   integer slots are in range.\n+8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n+   agreement of §5.3 (cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n+   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n+   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n+9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n+   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, store, module\n+   stores, result, children) and require the carried `requires` and\n+   `code` map to agree with it exactly: a declared segment not reached\n+   or a reached segment not declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Then\n+   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n+   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n+   with cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n+   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n+10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n+    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n+    never adds missing header keys.\n+11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n     the receiving composition.\n-11. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n+12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n     isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n     private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first,\n     then waits in order; adopt `max(local, carried)` for the id counter;\n@@ -443,10 +557,11 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n \n Diagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n-`[row-id slot-index …]`, `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n-and the lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` kinds, including the three this\n-carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata`,\n-`:noncanonical-cells`.\n+`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n+(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n+`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n+the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n+`:metadata`.\n \n ## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n \n@@ -462,12 +577,17 @@ table becomes the census.\n The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n \n - **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n-  version-3 state `S` within a supported profile,\n-  `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id, under a reconstruction context that\n-  preserves every carried canonical field: the id counter is compared as\n-  carried, not after `max`; occurrence, origin, cell bodies and op-ids\n-  are carried verbatim; resource remapping is undone by the lift's own\n-  numbering.\n+  version-3 state `S` in the semantic profile **for which `lower(S)`\n+  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n+  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n+  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n+  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n+  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n+  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n+  record, not minted; cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n+  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n+  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n+  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n - **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n@@ -477,12 +597,16 @@ The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   reference machine yields.\n-- **Legacy correspondence.** A version-0/1/2 body normalizes into\n-  version 3 with semantic preservation (same lowered machine under\n-  native equivalence); exact mutual inversion is required only between\n-  version 3 and its one canonical nested projection (§10.3), never\n-  against historical nested encodings with arbitrary sharing thresholds\n-  and cell names.\n+- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n+  their own version-aware readers, keeping their addresses and custody\n+  bindings (§10.8). Normalization into version 3 is a separate tool and\n+  is **partial**: a legacy body normalizes with semantic preservation\n+  (same lowered machine under native equivalence) or refuses with one\n+  of this carrier's named refusals (`:byte-string`, `:metadata`, a\n+  profile version 3 does not yet support); it never substitutes a\n+  version-3 address for an admitted legacy checkpoint. Exact mutual\n+  inversion is required only between version 3 and its one canonical\n+  nested projection (§10.3).\n \n The UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n Stage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n@@ -526,14 +650,30 @@ marked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n    establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n    translate between kernels.\n \n+Answers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n+\n+7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n+   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n+   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n+   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n+   addresses unchanged.\n+8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n+   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n+   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n+   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n+   one.\n+9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n+   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n+   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n+   topology, which is why §6 is now defined by least encoding over\n+   renamings with refinement as the algorithm.\n+\n Still open after this revision:\n \n-7. The stack, register and walker profile row tables (§2).\n-8. Whether a version-3 reader should accept version-2 bodies for lower\n-   only, or whether normalization (law 4) is a separate tool.\n-9. The exact cell-free skeleton encoding (§6): `[:cell nil]` as the\n-   placeholder is a proposal; the Architect should confirm it cannot\n-   collide with a literal.\n+10. The complexity bound of §6's individualization step on adversarial\n+    inputs (a lift is the emitter's own cost, but a receiver recomputes\n+    it; a receiver may need a work bound and a refusal for states whose\n+    symmetry exceeds it).\n \n ## 11. What this does not change\n \n@@ -542,8 +682,9 @@ pending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n the dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n private resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n custody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n-outcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds are\n-added, §8). The carrier is a new body version for the same facts. The\n+outcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n+`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n+version for the same facts. The\n Architect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n violates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n context edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\nd819f64104872244a6a1c2c87ea13df8ac0d053c\n     1\t# UCF transported state as tuples: the row carrier (body version 3)\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **Draft, proposed, not landed.** Written 2026-10-10 from the owner\n     4\tdiscussion recorded in\n     5\t`public/chp/blog/hygienic-parallel-transport-universal-continuation-format.blog`\n     6\tPart Three; revised the same day after the codex Architect review\n     7\t`collab/1791500000000-architect-ucf-transport-tuples-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n     8\t(round 1: thirteen findings; round 2: eight findings; all addressed\n     9\tbelow, and nine answers adopted in §10).\n    10\tSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), to UCF §7 in\n    11\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md),\n    12\tand to the version-2 amendment\n    13\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md),\n    14\twhose body is the fact list this carrier must reproduce. Follows the row\n    15\tconventions of [`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md).\n    16\tNothing here is implemented. Body versions 0, 1 and 2 keep their grammar,\n    17\treaders and bytes; this document proposes a fourth body version whose\n    18\tcarrier is rows, and says what it must preserve.\n    19\t\n    20\t## 1. Stance\n    21\t\n    22\tThree owner rulings fix the shape of this document.\n    23\t\n    24\t1. *Cross-VM safepoints are semantic-VM safepoints.* The Universal AST is\n    25\t   the canonical code; the instruction vector is its deterministic\n    26\t   lowering; a safepoint is `(segment-address, pc)` into that vector\n    27\t   (UCF §7.3.2, §7.4.1). This carrier changes nothing about code\n    28\t   identity, safepoints, or execution profiles.\n    29\t2. *The transported state is a tuple set.* Sharing and the literal/marker\n    30\t   distinction stop being special cases: a reference is a row id in a\n    31\t   typed position, sharing is Merkle, and a program's data is wrapped\n    32\t   where it can be confused with a reference. What does not collapse is\n    33\t   the per-entity decision between content and identity, which this\n    34\t   carrier makes a property of the row kind and then commits to the root\n    35\t   through a census.\n    36\t3. *Tuples are the transport form only.* They exist between a lift and a\n    37\t   lower. At rest they are rows like any other and queryable by the\n    38\t   ordinary interpreter. Nothing executes them; the destination lowers\n    39\t   them into its native representation.\n    40\t\n    41\tThis proposal chooses positional rows: a row kind fixes a slot list, one\n    42\ttable serves encoder and decoder, and a row's Merkle id commits to its\n    43\tchildren inline, as code rows already do. EAV is expressive enough and\n    44\tnothing in the invariants rules it out; positional rows are chosen for\n    45\tcompactness, closedness, and consistency with the existing row\n    46\tconventions, not because EAV was rejected by review.\n    47\t\n    48\t## 2. Positioning: body version 3, same profiles\n    49\t\n    50\tBody versions 0 and 1 (UCF §7.2.1) and 2 (the amendment) are nested\n    51\tcanonical-CBOR maps. **This carrier is body version 3.** The outer\n    52\t`:yin.k/version` gate of §7.2.1 and amendment §8 step 1 runs first and\n    53\tunchanged: a reader that does not speak 3 refuses with\n    54\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch` carrying the version found and the set it\n    55\tspeaks, before any row is read. A version-3 reader keeps versions 0, 1\n    56\tand 2 exactly as their contracts state and never upgrades a body across\n    57\tversions.\n    58\t\n    59\tThe execution profile is untouched. `:yin.k/contract` carries exactly the\n    60\tamendment §1 profile map `{:yin.k/engine e :yin.code/contract c\n    61\t:yin.k/version 1}`, from the same registry, under the same whole-tree\n    62\tgate (one profile per task and install tree). Code stamps `\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`,\n    63\t`\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a change of carrier,\n    64\tnot of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics, exactly the\n    65\tdistinction the amendment draws between its versions and executable\n    66\tcode.\n    67\t\n    68\tThis document tables the **semantic** profile's registers, frames and\n    69\tclosures in full, and **version 3 declares support for the semantic\n    70\tprofile only**: a version-3 body whose profile map names `:stack`,\n    71\t`:register` or `:walker` fails closed at the profile gate\n    72\t(`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) until those profiles' row tables and\n    73\tvalidation rules are published against amendment §5.2–5.4, with their\n    74\tcode addresses and profile maps unchanged. Like version 2,\n    75\tversion 3 transports native suspended state within a profile and does not\n    76\ttranslate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\n    77\timplements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\n    78\tis the semantic profile lowered through that engine's value profile, and\n    79\tvalue profiles are a separate document (§10.6).\n    80\t\n    81\t## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n    82\t\n    83\tEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n    84\t`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\n    85\tas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n    86\t\n    87\t**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\n    88\tsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n    89\t\n    90\t```clojure\n    91\t{:yin.k/handoff true\n    92\t :yin.k/version 3\n    93\t :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n    94\t :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n    95\t```\n    96\t\n    97\tUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\n    98\tany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so the gate's walk over install\n    99\tchildren is over a map from id to one row and is bounded by a visited\n   100\tset; a cref that leaves the rows, or a child graph that revisits a row,\n   101\tstops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable` rather than looping or choosing\n   102\tarbitrary data. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what is\n   103\thashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in `:rows`\n   104\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   105\t\n   106\t**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: the digest\n   107\tnamed by the address's own multihash prefix over the canonical CBOR of\n   108\tthe body, never an unspecified default. Equal bodies are one row. The id\n   109\tis Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n   110\t\n   111\t**Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\n   112\tin the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\n   113\tnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\n   114\tcarries it: a cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\n   115\tquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/c-1` are unrelated.\n   116\t\n   117\t**The census.** A cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\n   118\tare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\n   119\tcell commits to the cell's identity but not to what the cell holds. The\n   120\troot therefore carries a **cell census**, a content row\n   121\t\n   122\t```clojure\n   123\t[X :cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n   124\t[Bᵢ :cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n   125\t```\n   126\t\n   127\tthat maps every cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\n   128\tslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cell's contents. A\n   129\tchange to any cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\n   130\tthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\n   131\tauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\n   132\tlist exactly the cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\n   133\troot and per install child.\n   134\t\n   135\t**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n   136\t\n   137\t- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cell iref in a value, a\n   138\t  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n   139\t  and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n   140\t  (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n   141\t  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 5), because a receiver\n   142\t  validates, it does not trust construction.\n   143\t- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n   144\t  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n   145\t  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n   146\t  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n   147\t  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n   148\t  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n   149\t  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n   150\t  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n   151\t  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n   152\t  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n   153\t  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n   154\t\n   155\t## 4. The value union\n   156\t\n   157\tEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\n   158\ta two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n   159\t\n   160\t| `V` | Meaning |\n   161\t|---|---|\n   162\t| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n   163\t| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n   164\t| `[:cell C]` | a reference to the identity cell `C` |\n   165\t\n   166\tThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\n   167\tliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\n   168\tcan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n   169\t`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\n   170\tis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n   171\t\n   172\t**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\n   173\tarms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n   174\t`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n   175\t(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\n   176\tRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\n   177\tsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\n   178\tvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\n   179\tretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\n   180\tare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\n   181\tkind `:byte-string`.\n   182\t\n   183\t**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\n   184\tportability and whether a value reaches a reference: retained metadata\n   185\tthat holds a cursor reference, a closure, or any non-literal makes its\n   186\tcarrier non-literal, and since collection rows have no metadata slot,\n   187\tsuch a value refuses as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the\n   188\tpath (copying it as data would carry source coordinates and seals\n   189\tunremapped). Reference-free retained metadata on a literal travels as\n   190\tJing preserves it and is hashed as Jing hashes it. Metadata a reader\n   191\tstrips is not retained metadata and plays no part.\n   192\t\n   193\tA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\n   194\tdata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n   195\t\n   196\tA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\n   197\treference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\n   198\tholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\n   199\tand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n   200\t`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\n   201\tcollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\n   202\tdecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\n   203\tcollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n   204\t\n   205\t**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n   206\t§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\n   207\tguest literals, namely install responses, request and link envelopes,\n   208\toperation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data slot holds plain\n   209\tcanonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that governs it (an\n   210\tinstall response by the linker's delivery checks, an op-id by §7.4.3\n   211\tr5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these slots `D`.\n   212\t\n   213\tPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\n   214\tnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\n   215\ttheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n   216\t\n   217\t## 5. The row table\n   218\t\n   219\tKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\n   220\tnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\n   221\tnaming the row and slot. \"Sorted\" means ordered by Jing canonical-byte\n   222\torder of the sort key, and the sort key never contains a cell reference\n   223\t(§6). Ordered slots are part of the schema because they determine the\n   224\tMerkle id.\n   225\t\n   226\t`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\n   227\tcode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n   228\t`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\n   229\tonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\n   230\tof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n   231\t`:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\n   232\thave unique keys under canonical bytes **and** under portable decoded\n   233\tequality, since Jing's own map and set rules do not apply to a key list\n   234\tspelled as a vector.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n   237\t\n   238\t| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n   239\t|---|---|---|\n   240\t| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n   241\t| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n   242\t| `:set` | `V …` sorted by the element's cell-free skeleton (§6) | set |\n   243\t| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton | map |\n   244\t| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n   245\t| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n   246\t| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n   247\t| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n   248\t| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n   249\t| `:reified` | `regs-cref` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`: a captured continuation, no wait |\n   250\t| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates `R` |\n   251\t\n   252\t`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n   253\t§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\n   254\treceiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\n   255\treconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's\n   256\tregisters are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\n   257\tmust be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\n   258\tsafepoint.\n   259\t\n   260\t### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n   261\t\n   262\t| Kind | Slots |\n   263\t|---|---|\n   264\t| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n   265\t| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n   266\t| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n   267\t| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n   268\t| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n   269\t\n   270\t`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\n   271\telse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\n   272\twith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\n   273\tdirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\n   274\tsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\n   275\ttype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\n   276\town table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\n   277\treturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\n   278\tfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n   279\t(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n   280\t\n   281\t### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n   282\t\n   283\tOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\n   284\tslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\n   285\tthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n   286\t`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   287\t\n   288\t| reason | slots after the reason |\n   289\t|---|---|\n   290\t| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n   291\t| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n   292\t| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n   293\t| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  request-envelope-D  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n   294\t| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n   295\t| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n   296\t| `:install` | `name-S` |\n   297\t\n   298\tCorrelation must agree, not merely be present: a cell's `:cell-body`\n   299\tstream is the pending's response stream; an envelope's link id or call\n   300\tid equals the pending's; a `:link-request` envelope names the pending's\n   301\tmodule. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\n   302\tpresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\n   303\twriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\n   304\tand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n   305\t`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\n   306\twhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\n   307\tretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\n   308\tAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\n   309\tslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n   310\t`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\n   311\tis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n   312\t\n   313\t### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n   314\t\n   315\t| Kind | Slots |\n   316\t|---|---|\n   317\t| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n   318\t| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n   319\t| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n   320\t| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n   321\t| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n   322\t| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n   323\t\n   324\tResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\n   325\tthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\n   326\tM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\n   327\tsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\n   328\treachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n   329\t§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\n   330\tcontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\n   331\tchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\n   332\tmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\n   333\tthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\n   334\tid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\n   335\tis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\n   336\tcontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\n   337\tamendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n   338\t\n   339\t### 5.5 The root\n   340\t\n   341\t```clojure\n   342\t[T :task\n   343\t   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   344\t   contract         ; the profile map, plain  (amendment §1)\n   345\t   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   346\t   role             ; :root | :child\n   347\t   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   348\t   store-cref\n   349\t   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   350\t   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   351\t   cells-cref       ; :cells census\n   352\t   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map, unchanged\n   353\t   code             ; plain: {A code-payload …}, each payload verified to hash to its key (§7.2.1)\n   354\t   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   355\t   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   356\t   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   357\t   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   358\t   custody]         ; plain, see below\n   359\t```\n   360\t\n   361\tKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\n   362\tparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\n   363\tnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\n   364\tneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\n   365\tnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\n   366\tordered waits.\n   367\t\n   368\t`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n   369\t§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n   370\t\n   371\t| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n   372\t|---|---|---|---|\n   373\t| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n   374\t| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n   375\t| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n   376\t| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n   377\t| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n   378\t\n   379\tThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\n   380\tempty-header fork representation: a version-3 fork root is admitted\n   381\texactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and is never upgraded\n   382\tto custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n   383\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\n   384\tunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n   385\t\n   386\tThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\n   387\taddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\n   388\tare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\n   389\tplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\n   390\tkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\n   391\tthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n   392\t\n   393\t## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n   394\t\n   395\tCell ids must be a function of the state, or two lifts of one state give\n   396\ttwo root ids. Local rules are not enough: a cell's number must depend on\n   397\teverything that distinguishes it, including references to it from\n   398\telsewhere in the task (the review's counterexample: `'a` bound to a set\n   399\tholding cells X and Y with equal bodies, `'b` bound to X; any rule that\n   400\tlooks only at the set and the bodies cannot tell X from Y, but `'b`\n   401\tcan). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\n   402\ttopology** of the task, and the definition is the reference against\n   403\twhich any algorithm is checked.\n   404\t\n   405\t**Definition.** Let the task's cells be `n` in number. For each bijection\n   406\t`σ` from the cells to `{1 … n}`, build the complete row set of §5 with\n   407\tcell `c` spelled `:yin.k/c-σ(c)` (ordered containers in order; sets,\n   408\tmaps, stores, bindings and tables sorted by the canonical bytes of their\n   409\tkeys *as spelled under σ*), and take the canonical bytes of the root\n   410\tbody. The canonical numbering is the `σ` whose root bytes are least\n   411\tunder bytewise order. Two `σ` with equal least bytes are automorphisms\n   412\tof the task (they produce identical row sets by construction) and the\n   413\tchoice between them is immaterial by definition, not by assumption.\n   414\t\n   415\t**Algorithm.** The lift computes the same `σ` without enumerating all\n   416\t`n!` bijections:\n   417\t\n   418\t1. **Refinement.** Give every cell the signature of its body (stream,\n   419\t   position). Repeat: encode the task with each cell spelled by its\n   420\t   current signature class, in a fixed traversal (waits in order; within\n   421\t   a wait registers then pending; env bindings by symbol, stack\n   422\t   bottom-up, k bottom-to-top; parked table by `P`; store by key;\n   423\t   module stores by `M`; result; installs by name with each child as\n   424\t   its own task), and give each cell the new signature *(old class, the\n   425\t   multiset of positions at which it is referenced, where a position is\n   426\t   the path from the root with unordered containers' elements named by\n   427\t   their own current encoding)*. Stop when the partition into classes\n   428\t   stops changing. This is colour refinement over the reference graph\n   429\t   and terminates in at most `n` rounds.\n   430\t2. **Individualization.** If every class is a singleton, order classes\n   431\t   by their signature's canonical bytes and assign `1 … n`. Otherwise\n   432\t   pick the smallest class under that order, try each of its members as\n   433\t   the next individualized cell, refine again, and recurse; among the\n   434\t   resulting complete numberings take the one with the least root\n   435\t   bytes. Residual classes after full refinement are orbits of the\n   436\t   task's automorphism group, so every choice within a class yields the\n   437\t   same root bytes and the search is bounded by the symmetry of the\n   438\t   state, which for a parked task is small.\n   439\t\n   440\tSkeletons used inside the refinement keep the full value-union tags:\n   441\ta cell position is spelled `[:cell <class>]`, never a bare class, so it\n   442\tcannot collide with `[:lit [:cell …]]` (§10.9). Context lookups (parked\n   443\tids, store-of) are not expanded during refinement; they are followed\n   444\tonce as edges to their target rows, which are already in the traversal.\n   445\t\n   446\t**Validation.** The receiver recomputes `σ` from the decoded rows and\n   447\trefuses with `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:noncanonical-cells` if the\n   448\tcarried numbering differs; contiguity alone is not canonicality. This\n   449\tis a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift kind.\n   450\t\n   451\tUnder this rule two lifts of one state are byte-identical. Two lifts of\n   452\tdifferent states sharing a closure over the same cell may number it\n   453\tdifferently and so not share that closure's row across lifts; that\n   454\tcross-lift dedup is knowingly given up for identity-bearing values and\n   455\tnever affected rows below a cell. On lower, each `C` becomes one fresh\n   456\tprivate resource entry seeded at its carried position; every `[:cell C]`\n   457\tmaps to that entry; two references to one cell share it and one\n   458\treference per cell keeps its own (UCF §7.5.3).\n   459\t\n   460\t## 7. Equality and the content address\n   461\t\n   462\tThe transported state is a DAG rooted at the `:task` row. Its address is\n   463\tthe root id, which commits to every reachable content row, to every cell\n   464\tid through the references, and to every cell's contents through the\n   465\tcensus. Two transported states are equal iff their root ids are equal,\n   466\tand under §6 that holds iff they were lifted from states equal up to the\n   467\trenaming of cell ids that §6 makes canonical. No physical enumeration of\n   468\tthe row set needs an order, because the root commits to everything and\n   469\tthe pack, if one is addressed, has its own canonical encoding (§10.2).\n   470\tJing supplies canonical bytes and the digest of each body; this document\n   471\tsupplies the order within each body and the numbering of cells. Jing is\n   472\tasked to sort nothing it does not already sort.\n   473\t\n   474\tOccurrence and origin are inside the hash, as in versions 1 and 2 where\n   475\tthe body address is the hash of the whole body. Snapshot variants of one\n   476\toccurrence may therefore have distinct addresses while remaining one\n   477\toccurrence, exactly as UCF §7.3.4 intends.\n   478\t\n   479\t## 8. Validation on lower\n   480\t\n   481\tValidation is pure until the last step and checks the whole tree before\n   482\tone attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n   483\t\n   484\t1. **Gate.** Decode the wire map's canonical bytes retaining numeric\n   485\t   classes; the tag is present and the outer version is the exact\n   486\t   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\n   487\t   set). Reject duplicate row ids and a `:yin.k/root` that names no\n   488\t   `:task` row (`:yin.k/undecodable`). Walk install children from the\n   489\t   root in canonical module-name order with a visited set, following\n   490\t   only `:install` child crefs; a cref outside the rows, a revisit, or a\n   491\t   non-`:task` child stops the gate as `:yin.k/undecodable`. Gate every\n   492\t   child's version, then every profile map against the registry (only\n   493\t   the semantic profile is supported, §2), then require all versions 3\n   494\t   and all profiles equal to the root's. Malformed task structure is\n   495\t   `:yin.k/undecodable`; an unsupported version or profile is\n   496\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\n   497\t2. **Structure.** Every row's kind is tabled with the right arity; every\n   498\t   structural slot holds its tabled kind; every `[:row B]` targets a row\n   499\t   of a kind admissible in that position; every `V` is in §4's union,\n   500\t   every literal in the portable domain, every `D` in the canonical\n   501\t   bytes domain; exactly one row has role `:root`; kind rules of §5.5\n   502\t   hold; the custody slot matches the role, kind and policy table.\n   503\t3. **Canonical form.** Every sorted slot is in its tabled order; every\n   504\t   keyed row has unique keys under canonical bytes and under portable\n   505\t   decoded equality; optional absence has its one spelling (nil in the\n   506\t   tabled slot); a reference-free collection is spelled `[:lit d]`, never\n   507\t   a row; empty collections are literals; metadata obeys §4. A breach is\n   508\t   `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row and slot. Without this step two\n   509\t   different valid encodings could lower to one state, or a `:bindings`\n   510\t   row could carry one symbol twice and lose a binding on lower.\n   511\t4. **Hashes.** Every content row's id equals the address of its body\n   512\t   under the digest the id's multihash names; every code payload hashes\n   513\t   to its key `A` and is well formed under the stamp; the requested body\n   514\t   address, if one was given, equals the root id. Mismatch is\n   515\t   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the row or code key.\n   516\t5. **Graph.** The structural edge family is acyclic; every row is\n   517\t   reachable from the root through structural edges (an unreachable row\n   518\t   is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a census entry and\n   519\t   every census entry is reached **through the task's own roots, the\n   520\t   census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and per child; every\n   521\t   `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked table; every\n   522\t   `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every `:install`\n   523\t   pending has its entry and every entry's child is a `:child` task.\n   524\t6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare\n   525\t   (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`).\n   526\t7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n   527\t   whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n   528\t   `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n   529\t   safepoint; a reified value's pc is a `:current-continuation`\n   530\t   successor; return pcs follow a non-tail call; stack bases are bounded\n   531\t   and nondecreasing; closure params agree with the addressed lambda;\n   532\t   integer slots are in range.\n   533\t8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n   534\t   agreement of §5.3 (cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n   535\t   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n   536\t   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n   537\t9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n   538\t   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, store, module\n   539\t   stores, result, children) and require the carried `requires` and\n   540\t   `code` map to agree with it exactly: a declared segment not reached\n   541\t   or a reached segment not declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Then\n   542\t   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n   543\t   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n   544\t   with cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n   545\t   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n   546\t10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n   547\t    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n   548\t    never adds missing header keys.\n   549\t11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n   550\t    the receiving composition.\n   551\t12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n   552\t    isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n   553\t    private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first,\n   554\t    then waits in order; adopt `max(local, carried)` for the id counter;\n   555\t    publish the machine only after every child succeeds; run, poll,\n   556\t    mint, append, close or initialize nothing during lower.\n   557\t\n   558\tDiagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n   559\t`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n   560\t`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n   561\t(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n   562\t`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n   563\tthe two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n   564\t`:metadata`.\n   565\t\n   566\t## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n   567\t\n   568\t**Lift** (reference machine): refuse unless quiescent; number cells (§6);\n   569\twalk waits, parked records, the slice, module stores, the result and\n   570\teach install child, emitting rows and the census; refuse by path as UCF\n   571\t§7.5.4 and §7.2.1 specify. The nested carrier's value table\n   572\t(`:yin.k/values`) has no counterpart, sharing being Merkle; its cell\n   573\ttable becomes the census.\n   574\t\n   575\t**Lower**: §8 in order, then restore.\n   576\t\n   577\tThe laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   578\t\n   579\t- **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n   580\t  version-3 state `S` in the semantic profile **for which `lower(S)`\n   581\t  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n   582\t  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n   583\t  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n   584\t  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n   585\t  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n   586\t  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n   587\t  record, not minted; cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n   588\t  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n   589\t  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n   590\t  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n   591\t- **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   592\t  `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   593\t  external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n   594\t  set and its order, the result, and the effect trace.\n   595\t- **Cross-engine.** Running the corpus to each safepoint on an engine\n   596\t  that implements canonical semantic-VM execution yields, under\n   597\t  controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   598\t  descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   599\t  reference machine yields.\n   600\t- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n   601\t  their own version-aware readers, keeping their addresses and custody\n   602\t  bindings (§10.8). Normalization into version 3 is a separate tool and\n   603\t  is **partial**: a legacy body normalizes with semantic preservation\n   604\t  (same lowered machine under native equivalence) or refuses with one\n   605\t  of this carrier's named refusals (`:byte-string`, `:metadata`, a\n   606\t  profile version 3 does not yet support); it never substitutes a\n   607\t  version-3 address for an admitted legacy checkpoint. Exact mutual\n   608\t  inversion is required only between version 3 and its one canonical\n   609\t  nested projection (§10.3).\n   610\t\n   611\tThe UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n   612\tStage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n   613\toracle for the first law.\n   614\t\n   615\t## 10. Questions the review answered, and what remains\n   616\t\n   617\tAnswers 1–6 are the codex Architect's (round 1); where the Architect\n   618\tmarked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n   619\t\n   620\t1. **Occurrence inside the hash.** Kept inside, following the governing\n   621\t   rule and binding custody context to the published checkpoint. An\n   622\t   occurrence-independent execution-state address is an owner-level\n   623\t   protocol decision; if ever pursued it must sit beside, not replace, a\n   624\t   fully authenticated checkpoint address, and it does not substitute\n   625\t   for the census.\n   626\t2. **Packing.** Individual immutable content rows plus a self-contained\n   627\t   transport pack. When a pack is addressed it gets its own address and\n   628\t   canonical encoding, distinct from the semantic root id; cell ids\n   629\t   remain root-scoped inside it. Same answer as code-as-tuples §2.1\n   630\t   should receive; the loader interface is resolved explicitly, not by\n   631\t   layout.\n   632\t3. **The nested grammar.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 remain compatibility\n   633\t   inputs. One canonical nested projection of version 3 is kept for\n   634\t   single-message channels and must stay exactly mutually inverse with\n   635\t   the rows. Rows are the carrier authority once specified. Retiring old\n   636\t   readers is an owner version-support decision.\n   637\t4. **Contract stamp.** The profile map and code contracts do not move; a\n   638\t   carrier change is a new body version with its own gate. Any change to\n   639\t   instruction or scheduler semantics would separately revise the\n   640\t   execution contract and is not proposed here.\n   641\t5. **Reified and parked.** Sharing immutable `:regs` rows is acceptable;\n   642\t   the two keep distinct outer kinds (§5.1) and distinct lowering. A\n   643\t   reified capture has no pending; a parked reference resolves in task\n   644\t   context.\n   645\t6. **Foreign-engine value profiles.** Required before any implementation\n   646\t   brief claims heterogeneous execution conformance, and not drafted\n   647\t   here: admitted values, numeric behaviour, key equality, metadata,\n   648\t   resource and profile requirements, refusal sets, and safepoint\n   649\t   reconstruction obligations. Slot losslessness alone does not\n   650\t   establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n   651\t   translate between kernels.\n   652\t\n   653\tAnswers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n   654\t\n   655\t7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n   656\t   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n   657\t   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n   658\t   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n   659\t   addresses unchanged.\n   660\t8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n   661\t   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n   662\t   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n   663\t   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n   664\t   one.\n   665\t9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n   666\t   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n   667\t   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n   668\t   topology, which is why §6 is now defined by least encoding over\n   669\t   renamings with refinement as the algorithm.\n   670\t\n   671\tStill open after this revision:\n   672\t\n   673\t10. The complexity bound of §6's individualization step on adversarial\n   674\t    inputs (a lift is the emitter's own cost, but a receiver recomputes\n   675\t    it; a receiver may need a work bound and a refusal for states whose\n   676\t    symmetry exceeds it).\n   677\t\n   678\t## 11. What this does not change\n   679\t\n   680\tCode identity, the contract stamp and profile registry, safepoints,\n   681\tpending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n   682\tthe dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n   683\tprivate resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n   684\tcustody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n   685\toutcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n   686\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n   687\tversion for the same facts. The\n   688\tArchitect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n   689\tviolates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n   690\tcontext edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n   691\tthey are.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '47,393p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | tail -150\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '47,393p' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md; git diff HEAD~1 HEAD -- docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md | tail -150\"","aggregated_output":"\n## 2. Positioning: body version 3, same profiles\n\nBody versions 0 and 1 (UCF §7.2.1) and 2 (the amendment) are nested\ncanonical-CBOR maps. **This carrier is body version 3.** The outer\n`:yin.k/version` gate of §7.2.1 and amendment §8 step 1 runs first and\nunchanged: a reader that does not speak 3 refuses with\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch` carrying the version found and the set it\nspeaks, before any row is read. A version-3 reader keeps versions 0, 1\nand 2 exactly as their contracts state and never upgrades a body across\nversions.\n\nThe execution profile is untouched. `:yin.k/contract` carries exactly the\namendment §1 profile map `{:yin.k/engine e :yin.code/contract c\n:yin.k/version 1}`, from the same registry, under the same whole-tree\ngate (one profile per task and install tree). Code stamps `\"v3\"`, `\"b2\"`,\n`\"r2\"` and the inner version 1 do not move: this is a change of carrier,\nnot of instruction, scheduler, or value semantics, exactly the\ndistinction the amendment draws between its versions and executable\ncode.\n\nThis document tables the **semantic** profile's registers, frames and\nclosures in full, and **version 3 declares support for the semantic\nprofile only**: a version-3 body whose profile map names `:stack`,\n`:register` or `:walker` fails closed at the profile gate\n(`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`) until those profiles' row tables and\nvalidation rules are published against amendment §5.2–5.4, with their\ncode addresses and profile maps unchanged. Like version 2,\nversion 3 transports native suspended state within a profile and does not\ntranslate between kernels. The foreign-engine case (an engine that\nimplements canonical semantic-VM execution in its own layout, UCF §7.4.2)\nis the semantic profile lowered through that engine's value profile, and\nvalue profiles are a separate document (§10.6).\n\n## 3. Rows, id spaces, and the census\n\nEvery row is `[id kind & slots]`; the stored payload is the body\n`[kind & slots]`; the id is an address envelope outside the hashed bytes,\nas for code rows (code-as-tuples §2.1).\n\n**The wire unit** is one closed map, so that the gate of §8 has\nsomething to run on before any row is interpreted:\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/handoff true\n :yin.k/version 3\n :yin.k/root    B          ; the root :task row's id\n :yin.k/rows    [[id kind & slots] …]}   ; every row of the tree, any order\n```\n\nUnlisted keys refuse. Duplicate ids among `:yin.k/rows` refuse before\nany traversal (`:yin.k/undecodable`), so the gate's walk over install\nchildren is over a map from id to one row and is bounded by a visited\nset; a cref that leaves the rows, or a child graph that revisits a row,\nstops the gate with `:yin.k/undecodable` rather than looping or choosing\narbitrary data. When a pack is addressed (§10.2) this map is what is\nhashed, and a `:yin.k/root` that does not name a `:task` row in `:rows`\nis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n**Content rows** have `id = (dao.jing/segment-key body)`: the digest\nnamed by the address's own multihash prefix over the canonical CBOR of\nthe body, never an unspecified default. Equal bodies are one row. The id\nis Merkle: it commits to every row id in the body.\n\n**Identity rows** are cells (UCF §7.5.3). A cell has a task-local id `C`\nin the form `:yin.k/c-<n>`, minted by the lift under §6's canonical\nnumbering, never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\ncarries it: a cell id means nothing outside its root, in a pool or a\nquery, and two tasks' `:yin.k/c-1` are unrelated.\n\n**The census.** A cell's *contents* (its stream marker and kept position)\nare not reachable through its id, so a content row that references a\ncell commits to the cell's identity but not to what the cell holds. The\nroot therefore carries a **cell census**, a content row\n\n```clojure\n[X :cells  C₁ B₁  C₂ B₂  …]          ; sorted by n; Bᵢ is a cref\n[Bᵢ :cell-body  stream-cref  position]\n```\n\nthat maps every cell id to the content row of its body. The census is a\nslot of the root, so the root id commits to every cell's contents. A\nchange to any cell's position or stream changes `Bᵢ`, the census, and\nthe root. Identity references preserve aliasing; the census\nauthenticates contents. The validation rules (§8) require the census to\nlist exactly the cells the task reaches: none missing, none extra, per\nroot and per install child.\n\n**Acyclicity.** Two edge families exist and are checked separately:\n\n- *Structural value edges*: a cref in a slot, a cell iref in a value, a\n  census entry. Merkle construction cannot build a cref cycle bottom-up,\n  and a `:cell-body` references only a stream (content) and a position\n  (data), so no cell closes a cycle. The receiver still checks\n  acyclicity over this family explicitly (§8 step 5), because a receiver\n  validates, it does not trust construction.\n- *Context lookup edges*: a `:parked-ref` row names a parked id `P`\n  resolved through the task's parked table; a closure's `store-of` names\n  a manifest address `M` resolved through the module stores; an\n  `:install` pending names a module whose entry holds a child body.\n  These may close cycles and that is legal: a module store that holds a\n  closure whose `store-of` is that same module is the ordinary shape of\n  a linked module, and self- or mutually-referencing parked records\n  resolve through the explicit `P` table exactly as amendment §6 states.\n  A guest value that would need a cycle through *structural* edges is\n  refused at lift as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:cyclic` naming the\n  path, unchanged from UCF §7.5.3.\n\n## 4. The value union\n\nEvery position that holds a guest value holds one **encoded value** `V`,\na two-element vector whose first element is a fixed tag:\n\n| `V` | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `[:lit d]` | a literal: `d` is in the portable literal domain below, carried by Jing as data |\n| `[:row B]` | a reference to the content row `B` (a closure, a collection row, a frame value, a primitive, a stream marker) |\n| `[:cell C]` | a reference to the identity cell `C` |\n\nThe union is disjoint by the tag, not by the shape or spelling of `d`: a\nliteral keyword that happens to equal a row address is `[:lit k]` and\ncan never be read as a reference; a literal vector that looks like a\n`[:row …]` pair is `[:lit [...]]`. No decision depends on content, which\nis the UCF §7.5.1 property restated for rows.\n\n**The portable literal domain** is UCF §7.5.1's scalar and collection\narms, closed under nesting, with exactly the numeric classes Jing and\n`value-encoder` already admit: nil, booleans, exact integers of any width\n(Jing major types 0/1, tags 2/3), float64 carriers, Jing's Decimal and\nRational carriers under Jing's normalization rules, strings, keywords,\nsymbols, and vectors, lists, sets and maps *all of whose elements, keys,\nvalues and retained metadata are in the domain*. Carrier classes are\nretained, never coerced (an integral float stays a float). Byte strings\nare not admitted by this carrier and refuse as `:yin.k/non-portable`\nkind `:byte-string`.\n\n**Metadata** is walked like any other part of a value when deciding both\nportability and whether a value reaches a reference: retained metadata\nthat holds a cursor reference, a closure, or any non-literal makes its\ncarrier non-literal, and since collection rows have no metadata slot,\nsuch a value refuses as `:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:metadata` naming the\npath (copying it as data would carry source coordinates and seals\nunremapped). Reference-free retained metadata on a literal travels as\nJing preserves it and is hashed as Jing hashes it. Metadata a reader\nstrips is not retained metadata and plays no part.\n\nA guest map is always wrapped, `[:lit m]` or a `:map` row, and is open\ndata inside the wrapper; structural rows are closed.\n\nA collection whose elements, keys, values or metadata include a\nreference is not a literal; it becomes a row (§5.1) and the position\nholds `[:row B]`. A collection that reaches no reference is a literal\nand **must** be `[:lit d]`: a reference-free `:vec`, `:list`, `:set` or\n`:map` row is a non-canonical spelling and refuses (§8). Empty\ncollections are therefore always literals. This is the only structural\ndecision the encoder makes and it is decided by one question, *does this\ncollection reach a reference?*, answered by walking it.\n\n**Structural data** is a second, separate domain: values that UCF\n§7.4.3 requires to be in the canonical-bytes domain but that are not\nguest literals, namely install responses, request and link envelopes,\noperation ids, link ids and call ids. A structural-data slot holds plain\ncanonical-bytes data and is validated by the rule that governs it (an\ninstall response by the linker's delivery checks, an op-id by §7.4.3\nr5), not by the literal domain. Table rows mark these slots `D`.\n\nPositions that hold structural data (addresses, pcs, symbols as binding\nnames, reasons, phases, ids) are typed by the row table and are not `V`;\ntheir kinds are fixed per slot, as for code rows.\n\n## 5. The row table\n\nKinds are closed. An unknown kind, a wrong arity, a slot whose content is\nnot of the tabled kind, or a `V` outside §4's union is `:yin.k/undecodable`\nnaming the row and slot. \"Sorted\" means ordered by Jing canonical-byte\norder of the sort key, and the sort key never contains a cell reference\n(§6). Ordered slots are part of the schema because they determine the\nMerkle id.\n\n`N` is an exact CBOR integer in `[0, 2^52−1]` (amendment §2). `A` is a\ncode address, `M` a manifest address, `P` a parked id, `C` a cell id,\n`S` a symbol, `D` structural data (§4). Each `[:row B]` position admits\nonly the row kinds its table entry names; a `[:row B]` whose target is\nof another kind refuses (§8). Keyed rows (`:bindings`, `:map`, `:store`,\n`:parked-table`, `:cells`, the installs and module-stores slots) must\nhave unique keys under canonical bytes **and** under portable decoded\nequality, since Jing's own map and set rules do not apply to a key list\nspelled as a vector.\n\n### 5.1 Values (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots | Decodes to |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:vec` | `V …` in order | vector |\n| `:list` | `V …` in order | list |\n| `:set` | `V …` sorted by the element's cell-free skeleton (§6) | set |\n| `:map` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …`, each `kᵢ` a `V`, sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton | map |\n| `:bindings` | `S₁ V₁ S₂ V₂ …` sorted by symbol | `{S V}` |\n| `:env` | `bindings-cref  store-of` | E: the named environment plus, when `store-of` is an `M`, `engine/store-of-key` (amendment §2) |\n| `:closure` | `segment-A  entry-pc-N  params  bindings-cref  store-of` | semantic closure (amendment §6 semantic arm); `params` is a plain vector of symbols checked against the addressed lambda |\n| `:prim` | `name-S` | the named primitive after the profile check (UCF §7.5.2); the profile lives in requires |\n| `:stream` | `identity  descriptor` | a stream marker; both plain, authenticated at lift and re-sealed at lower |\n| `:reified` | `regs-cref` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/registers R}`: a captured continuation, no wait |\n| `:parked-ref` | `parked-id-P` | `{:yin.k/tag :yin.k/frame :yin.k/parked-id P}`: resolves in the task's parked table; never duplicates `R` |\n\n`:reified` and `:parked-ref` keep the two outer discriminants amendment\n§6 requires and lower differently: a reified value becomes a\nreceiver-owned native continuation from `R`; a parked reference\nreconstructs the engine's parked value for `P`. A reified value's\nregisters are validated in captured-value context (amendment §7): its pc\nmust be a `:current-continuation` site's successor, not a wait or park\nsafepoint.\n\n### 5.2 Semantic registers, waits, frames (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:regs` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-cref  k-cref` |\n| `:stack` | `V …` bottom first |\n| `:k` | `kframe-cref …` bottom to top |\n| `:kframe` | `segment-A  pc-N  env-cref  stack-base-N` |\n| `:wait` | `regs-cref  pending-cref` |\n\n`:regs` is amendment §5.1's `R` for the semantic profile, and nothing\nelse: no reason, no pending. A wait is a `:wait` row pairing registers\nwith a pending; a reified value and a parked record reference `:regs`\ndirectly. `:kframe` is exactly the semantic return frame `{:type :return\nsegment pc env stack-base}`; the semantic profile has no other frame\ntype on the wire (walker effect frames belong to the walker profile's\nown table). Resume pc and return pc must lie inside their segment, the\nreturn pc immediately after a non-tail call, stack bases nondecreasing\nfrom outermost to innermost and each at most the enclosing stack length\n(amendment §5.1), all checked in §8.\n\n### 5.3 Pending (content rows)\n\nOne kind, `:pending`, whose first slot is the reason and whose remaining\nslots are fixed per reason. The reasons are UCF §7.4.3's closed union as\nthe amendment r5 required-keys list fixes them; `:park` and\n`:call-effect` are not reasons and refuse as `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n| reason | slots after the reason |\n|---|---|\n| `:next` | `cell-C` |\n| `:put` | `stream-cref  value-V  op-id-D` |\n| `:ffi` | `cell-C  call-id-D  op` (op may be nil) |\n| `:ffi-request` | `call-id-D  request-envelope-D  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  response-cell-C  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-request` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C  envelope-D  op-id-D` |\n| `:link-response` | `link-id-D  name-S  request-stream-cref  response-stream-cref  cell-C` |\n| `:install` | `name-S` |\n\nCorrelation must agree, not merely be present: a cell's `:cell-body`\nstream is the pending's response stream; an envelope's link id or call\nid equals the pending's; a `:link-request` envelope names the pending's\nmodule. `op-id` is `{:yin.k/occurrence P :yin.k/seq n}` as plain data or nil,\npresent exactly when the write was first attempted through a fenced\nwriter, carried verbatim, never minted or renumbered by lift or lower,\nand subject to every rule of §7.4.3 r5 (below the root's\n`:yin.k/next-op-seq`, unique across the task and its children, absent\nwhen the body has no origin). `request-envelope` and `envelope` are the\nretained requests, retried verbatim. `call-id` and `link-id` are plain.\nAn `:install` pending is never sufficient alone: the root's installs\nslot must hold the entry for `name`, else lift refuses\n`:yin.k/non-portable` kind `:incomplete-install` and a body carrying it\nis `:yin.k/undecodable`.\n\n### 5.4 Task context (content rows)\n\n| Kind | Slots |\n|---|---|\n| `:store` | `k₁ V₁ k₂ V₂ …` sorted by the key's cell-free skeleton; the isolated slice of UCF §7.6.2 |\n| `:module-store` | `manifest-M  store-cref` |\n| `:parked-table` | `P₁ regs-cref₁ P₂ regs-cref₂ …` sorted by P |\n| `:install` | `name-S  phase  parent-D  response-D  child-cref` |\n| `:cells` | the census, §3 |\n| `:cell-body` | `stream-cref  position` (position in the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` portable cursor domain) |\n\nResource entries never appear in `:store`; streams and cells lower into\nthe receiver's private resource table beside the program store (linker\nM4, `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.3). `:module-store` rows are the per-module\nsnapshots of §7.6.2's M4 paragraph. The parked table holds exactly the\nreachable records. An `:install` row carries the complete entry of\n§7.4.3 r5: phase `:running` or `:parked`, parent link id (nil where the\ncontract permits), the response verbatim as structural data, and the\nchild as a cref to a whole `:task` row in the child role. \"Verified\"\nmeans verified by the **receiver**, never trusted because it decodes:\nthe response's manifest names the module the row is keyed by, its link\nid equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\nis delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\ncontract and the child's profile agree (`yin.vm.linker.md` §5, §9;\namendment §2). A failure is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the row.\n\n### 5.5 The root\n\n```clojure\n[T :task\n   version          ; 3                       (gated first, §8)\n   contract         ; the profile map, plain  (amendment §1)\n   kind             ; :blocked | :parked | :halted\n   role             ; :root | :child\n   id-counter       ; N                       (UCF §7.6.3)\n   store-cref\n   module-stores    ; crefs sorted by M\n   parked-cref      ; :parked-table\n   cells-cref       ; :cells census\n   requires         ; plain: UCF §7.6.1's map, unchanged\n   code             ; plain: {A code-payload …}, each payload verified to hash to its key (§7.2.1)\n   installs         ; crefs to :install rows, sorted by module name\n   frames           ; crefs to :wait rows, IN WAIT ORDER   (:blocked, :parked)\n   parked-id        ; P or nil                              (:parked only)\n   result           ; V or nil                              (:halted only)\n   custody]         ; plain, see below\n```\n\nKind rules are §7.2.1's: `:blocked` has nonempty frames and neither\nparked-id nor result; `:parked` has frames (possibly empty), a parked-id\nnaming a key of the parked table, no result; `:halted` has a result and\nneither frames nor parked-id. Frames restore in carried order and are\nnever collapsed to one; a task with an empty ready queue may hold several\nordered waits.\n\n`custody` is the custody arm carried as plain data, under the rules of\n§7.2.1 and amendment §3, by role, kind and policy:\n\n| role | kind | policy | `custody` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| root | blocked / parked | exclusive | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` on every successor, absent on a first export |\n| root | blocked / parked | fork | `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/fork}` |\n| root | halted | exclusive | `{:yin.k/origin …}` only: a result is not a lease subject |\n| root | halted | fork | nil: a fork halt has no custody data and a first fork halt has no origin to carry |\n| child | any | — | nil; validated with its root's occurrence, origin and counter passed down |\n\nThe explicit fork policy spelling projects onto the amendment's\nempty-header fork representation: a version-3 fork root is admitted\nexactly as a version-0 body is, proposes nothing, and is never upgraded\nto custody; an exclusive-only receiver refuses it as\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. Admission semantics are the governing ones\nunchanged; only the spelling is new. No body carries an epoch.\n\nThe root is a content row and **its id is the transported state's\naddress**, the `B` of UCF §7.3.4 for this carrier. Occurrence and origin\nare inside it, as the governing rule keeps them (§10.1). The code map is\nplain data in the root rather than a row kind: each instruction vector\nkeeps its own address `A` computed exactly as UCF §7.3.2 computes it, and\nthe carrier verifies, never recomputes, that address.\n\n## 6. Canonical cell numbering\n+   is `:yin.k/undecodable`); every `[:cell C]` names a census entry and\n+   every census entry is reached **through the task's own roots, the\n+   census's declaration edges excluded**, per task and per child; every\n+   `:parked-ref` and `parked-id` resolves in the parked table; every\n+   `store-of` resolves to a carried module store; every `:install`\n+   pending has its entry and every entry's child is a `:child` task.\n+6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 and compare\n+   (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind `:noncanonical-cells`).\n+7. **Code and frames.** Each wait's resume pc is a static safepoint\n+   whose kinds admit the wait's reason (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n+   `:reason-mismatch`); a parked record's pc is an explicit-park\n+   safepoint; a reified value's pc is a `:current-continuation`\n+   successor; return pcs follow a non-tail call; stack bases are bounded\n+   and nondecreasing; closure params agree with the addressed lambda;\n+   integer slots are in range.\n+8. **Pending and operations.** Reason-specific slot rules; correlation\n+   agreement of §5.3 (cell stream, envelope ids, module names); every\n+   op-id below `next-op-seq`, unique across the tree, absent without\n+   origin; every install response verified as §5.4 requires.\n+9. **Dependency closure.** Compute the census of amendment §6 from the\n+   rows (waits, pendings and envelopes, parked records, store, module\n+   stores, result, children) and require the carried `requires` and\n+   `code` map to agree with it exactly: a declared segment not reached\n+   or a reached segment not declared is `:yin.k/undecodable`. Then\n+   evaluate `requires` per UCF §7.6.5: missing segments, primitives,\n+   modules, streams, cursor profiles are `:yin.k/unsatisfied`; a body\n+   with cells claims `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; module-store coverage is\n+   complete for every `store-of` reached.\n+10. **Custody.** For an exclusive root, the occurrence, baseline and\n+    operation inspector and admission checks of §7.7.8; an authority\n+    never adds missing header keys.\n+11. **Composition.** Protection, resource, primitive and grant inputs of\n     the receiving composition.\n-11. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n+12. **Restore.** Reconstruct isolated code spaces, fresh values, the\n     isolated store, module-store instances (first link wins), one\n     private resource entry per cell, the parked table, children first,\n     then waits in order; adopt `max(local, carried)` for the id counter;\n@@ -443,10 +557,11 @@ one attachment. The order follows amendment §8 and is binding:\n \n Diagnostics use the established families and no new top-level outcome:\n `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\n-`[row-id slot-index …]`, `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`, `:yin.k/unsatisfied`,\n-and the lift-side `:yin.k/non-portable` kinds, including the three this\n-carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string`, `:metadata`,\n-`:noncanonical-cells`.\n+`[row-id slot-index …]` and a `:yin.k/kind` where one is named above\n+(`:reason-mismatch`, `:noncanonical-cells`), `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`,\n+`:yin.k/unsatisfied`, and on the lift side `:yin.k/non-portable` with\n+the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF §7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and\n+`:metadata`.\n \n ## 9. Lift, lower, and the laws\n \n@@ -462,12 +577,17 @@ table becomes the census.\n The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n \n - **Canonical round trip.** For every valid, canonical (§6-numbered)\n-  version-3 state `S` within a supported profile,\n-  `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id, under a reconstruction context that\n-  preserves every carried canonical field: the id counter is compared as\n-  carried, not after `max`; occurrence, origin, cell bodies and op-ids\n-  are carried verbatim; resource remapping is undone by the lift's own\n-  numbering.\n+  version-3 state `S` in the semantic profile **for which `lower(S)`\n+  succeeds** (adequate composition, resource and admission inputs; a\n+  refusal is not a counterexample), and with no execution between lower\n+  and lift, `lift(lower(S)) = S` by root id under the **fresh-receiver\n+  reconstruction context**: the receiver's id counter is 0 so\n+  `max(local, carried)` is the carried value; occurrence, origin and the\n+  custody arm are supplied to the lift from the restored task's own\n+  record, not minted; cell bodies and op-ids are carried verbatim; the\n+  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\n+  (a receiver with a larger counter, or any step taken) the law does not\n+  apply and native equivalence is the claim.\n - **Native observational equivalence.** `lower(lift(m))` need not equal\n   `m` as native state (fresh ids, seals, private keys); under equivalent\n   external inputs it must preserve the next observable step, the wait\n@@ -477,12 +597,16 @@ The laws, with the domains the Architect's finding 12 requires:\n   controlled composition identities (occurrence, origin, resource\n   descriptors fixed by the harness), the same version-3 row set the\n   reference machine yields.\n-- **Legacy correspondence.** A version-0/1/2 body normalizes into\n-  version 3 with semantic preservation (same lowered machine under\n-  native equivalence); exact mutual inversion is required only between\n-  version 3 and its one canonical nested projection (§10.3), never\n-  against historical nested encodings with arbitrary sharing thresholds\n-  and cell names.\n+- **Legacy correspondence.** Versions 0, 1 and 2 are lowered directly by\n+  their own version-aware readers, keeping their addresses and custody\n+  bindings (§10.8). Normalization into version 3 is a separate tool and\n+  is **partial**: a legacy body normalizes with semantic preservation\n+  (same lowered machine under native equivalence) or refuses with one\n+  of this carrier's named refusals (`:byte-string`, `:metadata`, a\n+  profile version 3 does not yet support); it never substitutes a\n+  version-3 address for an admitted legacy checkpoint. Exact mutual\n+  inversion is required only between version 3 and its one canonical\n+  nested projection (§10.3).\n \n The UCF §7.11 harness gains one assertion per law. During transition the\n Stage D/E corpus, re-encoded through the canonical projection, is the\n@@ -526,14 +650,30 @@ marked a point as an owner decision it is labelled so.\n    establish execution parity, and version 3, like version 2, does not\n    translate between kernels.\n \n+Answers 7–9 are the Architect's (round 2) and are adopted:\n+\n+7. **Other profile tables.** Deferral is acceptable for a semantic-only\n+   Draft; version 3 declares semantic support only and the other\n+   profiles fail closed at the gate (§2) until their complete tables and\n+   validation rules are published, with their profiles and code\n+   addresses unchanged.\n+8. **Legacy readers.** Direct version-aware lowering of versions 0–2,\n+   preserving address and custody binding; normalization is a separate,\n+   explicit, partial tool (§9) and a normalized checkpoint has a\n+   different address that never silently replaces an admitted legacy\n+   one.\n+9. **Skeleton placeholder.** Safe only if the full value-union tag is\n+   retained in the skeleton, which §6 now requires (`[:cell <class>]`,\n+   never a bare class); and a placeholder does not canonicalize alias\n+   topology, which is why §6 is now defined by least encoding over\n+   renamings with refinement as the algorithm.\n+\n Still open after this revision:\n \n-7. The stack, register and walker profile row tables (§2).\n-8. Whether a version-3 reader should accept version-2 bodies for lower\n-   only, or whether normalization (law 4) is a separate tool.\n-9. The exact cell-free skeleton encoding (§6): `[:cell nil]` as the\n-   placeholder is a proposal; the Architect should confirm it cannot\n-   collide with a literal.\n+10. The complexity bound of §6's individualization step on adversarial\n+    inputs (a lift is the emitter's own cost, but a receiver recomputes\n+    it; a receiver may need a work bound and a refusal for states whose\n+    symmetry exceeds it).\n \n ## 11. What this does not change\n \n@@ -542,8 +682,9 @@ pending-wait semantics and the r5 required keys, install-child rules,\n the dependency closure and satisfaction check, the isolated store, the\n private resource table, module-store instancing, scheduler state, the\n custody header and admission rules, fencing and operation ids, the\n-outcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (three lift kinds are\n-added, §8). The carrier is a new body version for the same facts. The\n+outcome families, and the existing refusal kinds (two lift kinds and two\n+`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added, §8). The carrier is a new body\n+version for the same facts. The\n Architect's round-1 statement is the acceptance bar: tuple transport\n violates none of the six invariants once polymorphic references and\n context edges are specified and validated, and §4, §3 and §8 are where\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '688,726p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n 'request-envelope|dao.stream.apply/args' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc | tail -20; git rev-parse HEAD; sed -n '144,161p' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md; sed -n '2930,2960p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '688,726p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc; rg -n 'request-envelope|dao.stream.apply/args' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc | tail -20; git rev-parse HEAD; sed -n '144,161p' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md; sed -n '2930,2960p' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"    (cond-> {:yin.k/reason :ffi\n             :yin.k/call-id (entry-call-id (:engine (:v2 @found)) entry)\n             :yin.k/cell (cell-for! vm serve! found\n                                    (get-in entry [:cursor-ref :id]))}\n      (contains? entry :op) (assoc :yin.k/op (:op entry)))\n\n    :put\n    (do (when-not (contains? entry :datom)\n          (non-portable! :incomplete-write\n                         {:yin.k/pc (:pc entry)}))\n        {:yin.k/reason :put\n         :yin.k/stream (marker-for! vm serve! found (:stream-id entry))\n         :yin.k/value (encode (:datom entry))})\n\n    :ffi-request\n    (let [route (if (and (:response-cursor entry)\n                         (:response-stream entry)\n                         (:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids vm))\n                  {:id (:response-cursor entry)\n                   :stream-id (:response-stream entry)}\n                  (call-out-cell (:resources vm)))]\n      (if (nil? route)\n        (unsatisfied!)\n        (let [envelope (:datom entry)]\n          {:yin.k/reason :ffi-request\n           :yin.k/call-id (entry-call-id (:engine (:v2 @found)) entry)\n           :yin.k/request-envelope (encode envelope)\n           :yin.k/request-op (or (apply2/request-op envelope)\n                                 (:op entry)\n                                 (:op (:k entry)))\n           :yin.k/request-args (apply2/request-args envelope)\n           :yin.k/request (marker-for! vm serve! found\n                                       (:stream-id entry))\n           :yin.k/response (marker-for! vm serve! found\n                                        (:stream-id route))\n           :yin.k/response-cell (cell-for! vm serve! found\n                                           (:id route))})))\n\n    (:link-request :link-response)\n714:           :yin.k/request-envelope (encode envelope)\n1718:      (do (require-keys pending [:yin.k/call-id :yin.k/request-envelope\n2032:   :ffi-request [#{:yin.k/reason :yin.k/call-id :yin.k/request-envelope\n3223:                      :datom (decode (:yin.k/request-envelope pending))}\nd819f64104872244a6a1c2c87ea13df8ac0d053c\n## Encoding contract\n\nUse Boring's `:canonical` profile with string references, shaped arrays, and\noffset indexes disabled. Jing's normalization and named CBOR extensions are\nfixed, not backend-configurable. The hash covers only canonical payload\nbytes, never a backend coordinate, file frame, address envelope, or network\nmessage.\n\nSupported values are nil, booleans, strings, keywords, symbols, byte strings,\nvectors, finite lists/sequences, maps, sets, metadata, integers,\nfloating-point numbers, big integers, decimals, and ratios. \"Supported\" is\nqualified: it means the encoder accepts the value once its metadata is\nhost-clean. On Dart, a runtime-created list can carry an unsupported\nconstructor `:tag` (a Dart Type), and internal producers such as the VM's\nreader-position projection can reattach such metadata. Reject arbitrary\nrecords, functions, and unsupported host objects before storage. The\nnumeric carriers described below are explicitly supported exceptions to\nthe record restriction; arbitrary Boring records do not become Jing\n\n        :dao.stream.apply/eval-operand\n        (assoc base :frame {:op (:op node)\n                            :operands (:operands node)\n                            :evaluated (mapv decode (:yin.k/evaluated kw))})\n\n        :eval-stream-put-val\n        (assoc base\n               :frame node\n               :stream-ref (decode (:yin.k/stream-ref kw)))\n\n        :dao.stream.apply/request-sent\n        (assoc base :parked-id (:yin.k/parked-id kw) :op (:yin.k/op kw))\n\n        :dao.stream.apply/eval-call\n        (cond-> base\n          (contains? kw :yin.k/call-id) (assoc :call-id (:yin.k/call-id kw)))\n\n        :eval-define (assoc base :name (:yin.k/name kw))\n        :eval-resume-val (assoc base :parked-id (:yin.k/parked-id kw))))))\n\n\n(defn- decode-registers-v2\n  \"One version-2 register map back in the receiver's native coordinates:\n   segments through the alias column of the attached code (`ctx`'s\n   `:aliases`), or the layout rebuilt from the carried `:code`, values\n   decoded and re-sealed under the receiving task.\"\n  [engine decode {:keys [aliases code node-of]} r]\n  (case engine\n    :walker\n    {:env (decode-env-v2 decode (:yin.k/env r))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The earlier alias counterexample now separates during refinement, but the algorithm does not establish the stated least-root-byte definition. Refinement classes also need not be automorphism orbits. I found two further regressions: the gate rejects shared identical child-task rows, and raw FFI envelopes bypass recursive guest-value encoding.\n"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-09 17:35:05 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-10 00:35:05 Asia/Bangkok\n\nReviewed `d819f64104872244a6a1c2c87ea13df8ac0d053c`, the requested diff and revised sections. No files edited.\n\nThe census, value union, custody correction, and most validation additions pass. The canonical-labeling algorithm still does not implement its definition, and two corrections introduce transport regressions.\n\n1. **HIGH — architectural defect | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:430 | The algorithm does not compute the minimum over all bijections.** If two cells have different bodies, their initial classes are already singletons. The algorithm assigns numbers by signature order and evaluates only that numbering. The definition considers both permutations. Signature order does not imply least root-body bytes: those bytes contain Merkle addresses, whose lexical order does not preserve the ordering of the hashed signatures. Individualization explores only residual-class permutations, so it cannot repair this omission. | Keep exhaustive minimization as the reference definition and permit pruning only with a proof that it preserves that minimum. Alternatively, define the canonical representative through a fully specified invariant refinement/search procedure, rather than claiming it equals the unrestricted minimum. These are different definitions.\n\n2. **HIGH — incorrect architectural argument | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:435 | Stable refinement classes need not be automorphism orbits.** Encode a graph as a guest set of ordered pairs of equal-body cells, including both directions of every edge. A disjoint triangle and square give every cell two first-position and two second-position occurrences under identical class-encoded edge paths. Refinement leaves all seven cells together, but no automorphism exchanges a triangle vertex with a square vertex. Thus unresolved cells can be distinguishable without refinement distinguishing them. | Remove the orbit and “small symmetry” claims. Individualization must explore unresolved alternatives unless a separate automorphism proof permits pruning. Complexity depends on the search, not merely the actual automorphism group.\n\n3. **HIGH — transport regression | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:490 | A global revisit is not necessarily a cycle.** Two different install entries can have identical child-task content, especially identical halted children. Merkle deduplication then gives them the same child cref. The global visited-set gate rejects the second occurrence, although the structural graph is a valid DAG and the installs represent separate native task instances. | Detect cycles with an active recursion-path set. Cache completed content validation separately. Restore each install occurrence as its own task instance, with task-local cells and contextual custody validation; sharing immutable child content must not merge mutable native tasks.\n\n4. **HIGH — transport regression | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:292 | A retained FFI envelope is not wholly structural data.** Its arguments can contain guest values. The existing encoder recursively encodes the retained envelope (`handoff.cljc:714`), and lowering decodes it before retry. Making the entire envelope raw `D` can reject portable closures or preserve source resource ids/seals as ordinary maps, bypassing cell census and dependency discovery. The structural-data split is appropriate for install responses, but too broad for the whole FFI envelope. | Preserve a recursive encoded-value envelope, or specify a closed envelope row with structural correlation fields and `V`-encoded argument fields. Reconstruct the retained request without recomputing its guest arguments. Keep raw `D` for genuinely structural payloads.\n\n5. **HIGH — specification inconsistency | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:241 | The candidate encoding has conflicting ordering rules.** The row table still sorts sets/maps/stores by cell-free skeleton; §5 says sort keys never contain cell references; §6 now sorts keys as spelled under each candidate renaming. Skeleton ties therefore still lack a unique tabled row order, while validation requires “its tabled order.” | Use one definitive ordering rule for emitted candidate rows and receiver canonical-form validation. Reserve class-erased skeletons for refinement only. Specify recursive class encoding without retaining cell-dependent row hashes, and fix the content-row emission algorithm policy; dispatching verification by each supplied address does not itself select a unique canonical address scheme.\n\n6. **MEDIUM — specification gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:183 | Row-backed collections with literal metadata remain uncovered.** The text refuses metadata that contains references. A vector containing a cursor and carrying ordinary metadata such as `{:doc \"x\"}` nevertheless needs a collection row, which has no metadata slot. Its metadata is neither preserved nor explicitly refused by the revised rule. | Refuse every collection requiring a row when it has retained metadata, or add a specified metadata representation. Continue preserving reference-free metadata on literal collections. The refusal restriction is a proposed carrier limitation, not an inherited UCF requirement.\n\n7. **MEDIUM — validation-order gap | docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:503 | Recursive canonical-form checks precede reference resolution and acyclicity.** Portable decoded-key equality and determining whether a collection reaches references require traversing referenced values. Malformed input can contain missing references or cycles before step 5 has established traversal safety. Set-element uniqueness is also absent from the keyed-row list, despite the same decoded-collapse risk. | Establish reference completeness and structural acyclicity before recursive canonical-form checks, or explicitly make those earlier checks bounded and cycle-safe. Require set-element uniqueness under canonical bytes and portable decoded equality. This remains pure data validation before restoration.\n\nOn §6 specifically: the `'a → #{X Y}, 'b → X` counterexample does separate in the first refinement round—X has the additional distinguished reference path. The triangle/square construction above shows that separation is not complete. Retaining the old class makes refinement monotone: for `n > 0`, there are at most `n−k` strict splitting rounds from `k` initial classes, plus a final stability check, so “at most n rounds” is defensible for **partition stabilization**. It is not a bound on individualization or on signature-processing work.\n\nThe least-encoding definition can be made well-formed: a finite set of bijections has a minimum once candidate encoding, digest policy, and child-task handling are fixed. Equal complete commitments then make minimizing ties immaterial under the usual hash-collision assumption. The current algorithm does not establish that minimum.\n\nFor §10.10, require a receiver work bound before an implementation brief. Bound decoded bytes, rows, cells, traversal depth, refinement work, and search nodes; elapsed time can be an additional operational limit. Exceeding a local budget must produce a structured resource/capability refusal before attachment, not label a valid state `:noncanonical-cells`. Never fall back to encounter order or accept an unfinished canonicality check. The exact limits and refusal mapping remain implementation/composition decisions; they must not alter canonical bytes.\n\nProperties that passed:\n\n- The census commits to cell contents and preserves distinct-versus-shared cell identity.\n- Full value-union tags distinguish placeholders and references from literal data.\n- Decimal/Rational support and retained-metadata traversal address the prior numeric and metadata-reference omissions.\n- Semantic-only support, outer version gating, explicit root framing, and duplicate-id rejection are appropriate.\n- Both halted custody arms are represented; the fork spelling preserves the governing admission behavior.\n- Captured-continuation eligibility, install-response verification, correlation agreement, dependency-census agreement, and census usage excluding declaration edges are now explicit.\n- Successful-lower, fresh-receiver round-trip domains and partial legacy normalization are materially improved.\n- Transport/query interpretation remains separate from native execution. The approach introduces no required hidden global state, callbacks, implicit control flow, or shared mutable native state.\n\nThe new carrier version, canonical representative, pack format, explicit fork spelling, semantic-only initial scope, and metadata/byte-string restrictions are **design proposals**, not consequences uniquely dictated by the owner’s three rulings or the six invariants. They may be published as proposed choices once internally sound; implementation acceptance should identify them explicitly. No additional owner-level change to execution or custody semantics is needed to correct the findings above.\n\nStep 12 is not yet justified as safe for every accepted input because of findings 3, 4, and 7. The overall validation phase structure is otherwise suitable.\n\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED — correct findings 1–5 before publishing the Draft; complete 6–7 and the §10.10 work-bound contract before the implementation brief."}}
